INT. CASTLE HOTEL - TOWER SUITE - NIGHT

A round room papered in faded roses. A four-poster bed, a dark window, a candle guttering on the sill.

OTTILIE PRAHL, 30s, in a travel-stained dress, sits on the edge of the bed with her hands pressed between her knees. A MAN, 40s, in a doorman's greatcoat, stands in the doorway, his face half in shadow.

MAN
Your room, madam.

OTTILIE
It's the wrong room.

MAN
It is the room you were given.

She says nothing. She looks at the window, then at his hands, which are clean.

OTTILIE
You carried my bag up four flights and you're not out of breath.

MAN
I am used to stairs.

OTTILIE
What's your name?

He considers.

MAN
Calder.

OTTILIE
Calder. And is there someone else in the hotel tonight?

CALDER
Only the guest in the east wing.

OTTILIE
Who is that?

Calder steps inside and closes the door. The candle leans.

CALDER
Someone who has been asking for you since Tuesday.

Ottilie rises. She does not rush. She crosses to the window and lifts the latch.

OTTILIE
Tell him I've been here all along.

CALDER
He knows.

Behind him, in the hall, a floorboard creaks under a heavy tread.
